@* Debounced search input.

   THE PROBLEM IT SOLVES: every page here used `@@bind:event="oninput"`, which in Blazor Server means a
   WebSocket round-trip to the server on EVERY KEYSTROKE - the server then re-filters the list, re-renders
   both the table and the card list, and ships a DOM diff back. Over Tailscale from a phone that is one
   full network round-trip per character, and it is exactly why typing felt like the app was thinking
   about something else.

   Two changes fix it:

   1. DEBOUNCE (300ms). The keystroke still reaches the server, but the expensive part - re-filtering and
      re-rendering the list - only runs once the user pauses. 300ms is the widely used figure: long
      enough to swallow a burst of typing, short enough that the result still feels immediate.

   2. THE INPUT IS NOT TWO-WAY BOUND. `value` is written once, at first render, and never re-sent from
      server state afterwards. That matters more than the debounce: with @@bind, the server echoes the
      value back and Blazor writes it into the DOM, so on a slow link characters can visibly reorder or
      get eaten mid-word. Left unbound, the browser's own input handling stays in charge and typing is
      always instant regardless of latency - the only thing that waits is the filtered list.

   The cancellation is deliberately per-keystroke: each new character cancels the pending timer, so N
   fast characters produce exactly one filter pass, not N. *@
@implements IDisposable

<div class="search-field">
    <Icon Name="search" Size="16" />
    <input type="search"
           placeholder="@Placeholder"
           aria-label="@(AriaLabel ?? Placeholder)"
           value="@_initialValue"
           @oninput="OnInputAsync" />
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>Initial text only - this is NOT re-applied on later renders, by design (see the note above).</summary>
    [Parameter] public string Value { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    [Parameter] public EventCallback<string> ValueChanged { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public string Placeholder { get; set; } = "ค้นหา...";
    [Parameter] public string? AriaLabel { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Quiet period before the search actually runs. Raise it for a filter that hits the Server
    /// (Audit Log queries by GUID), leave it for one that filters an already-loaded list.</summary>
    [Parameter] public int DelayMs { get; set; } = 300;

    private string _initialValue = string.Empty;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _debounce;

    protected override void OnInitialized() => _initialValue = Value;

    private async Task OnInputAsync(ChangeEventArgs e)
    {
        var text = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty;

        _debounce?.Cancel();
        _debounce?.Dispose();
        _debounce = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _debounce.Token;

        try
        {
            await Task.Delay(DelayMs, token);
        }
        catch (TaskCanceledException)
        {
            return; // a newer keystroke arrived - that one owns the search now
        }

        if (token.IsCancellationRequested) return;
        await ValueChanged.InvokeAsync(text);
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        // Without this, closing a page mid-type leaves a timer that resolves into a disposed component.
        _debounce?.Cancel();
        _debounce?.Dispose();
    }
}
